---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHeader from "../components/PageHeader.astro";
import { HOSTING, LEGAL, SYNC, WEBSITE_REPO, docs } from "../site";

const privacyMail = `mailto:${LEGAL.privacyEmail}`;
---

<Base
  title="Privacy policy"
  description="What this website and the Magnetite browser do with your data: no cookies, visitor statistics on our own server, no telemetry in the browser."
>
  <PageHeader eyebrow="Legal" title="Privacy policy">
    <p class="mt-5 font-mono text-xs tracking-wide text-muted">Last updated: 10 November 2026</p>
  </PageHeader>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pt-12">

    <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-6 leading-relaxed text-secondary">
      <p class="font-semibold text-fg">In short</p>
      <p class="mt-2">
        This website sets no cookies. It counts visits with Umami, an open-source tool we run on our own servers in
        {HOSTING.location}, without storing your IP address. The Magnetite browser sends no telemetry and needs no account.
        The details follow.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose mt-4">
      <h2 id="controller">1. Who is responsible</h2>
      <p>
        Responsible for this website (the controller under the GDPR) is {LEGAL.entity}, {LEGAL.street}, {LEGAL.city},
        {LEGAL.country}. For anything about your data, write to <a href={privacyMail}>{LEGAL.privacyEmail}</a>. See also
        the <a href="/imprint/">imprint</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="website">2. This website</h2>
      <h3>What it does not do</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          No cookies and no other storage in your browser. The site’s own scripts only run its animations and the live demo
          of the sidebar; they send nothing and store nothing.
        </li>
        <li>No advertising, no tracking pixels, no tracking across other websites.</li>
        <li>
          No content from other companies. Text, images, styles, scripts and the one web font come from this server; the
          one exception is the statistics script described below, which comes from our own statistics server. There are no
          embedded videos, maps or social media buttons. The live demo shows sample pages that are part of this site; it
          loads no website.
        </li>
        <li>
          No forms. We do not collect anything you type; what you type into the live demo (its command bar, its archive
          search) stays in the page.
        </li>
        <li>
          Links to other sites, such as GitHub, are plain links: the other site is contacted only if you follow one, and our
          pages ask your browser not to tell it where you came from.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="statistics">Visitor statistics</h3>
      <p>
        To see how many people visit this site and which pages they read, we use Umami, an open-source analytics tool that
        we run ourselves on our own infrastructure in {HOSTING.location}.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Umami records the page’s address and title, the referring page if your browser sends one, your screen size and
          your browser’s language.
        </li>
        <li>
          From the request it works out your browser, operating system and device type, and an approximate location
          looked up from your IP address (at most country, region and city).
        </li>
        <li>
          Your IP address is not stored. Umami sets no cookies and stores nothing in your browser. To count several page
          views of one visitor as one visit, it computes a hash that changes regularly and cannot be turned back into the
          data it was made from.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Legal basis: Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR. Our legitimate interest is to learn which pages are useful and to improve the site.
        The data is processed and stored in {HOSTING.location} only; we do not combine it with other data and do not pass it
        on. You can object to this processing at any time (Art. 21 GDPR): write to
        <a href={privacyMail}>{LEGAL.privacyEmail}</a>. If a content blocker stops Umami’s script, the site works the same.
      </p>

      <h3>Hosting and server logs</h3>
      <p>
        The site and the statistics server run on our own infrastructure, on servers of a hosting provider in
        {HOSTING.location} that processes data on our behalf (Art. 28 GDPR). When you open a page, your browser sends the
        usual request data: your IP address, date and time, the address requested, the referring page if your browser sends
        one, and your browser’s user agent. We keep these in server logs to deliver the site and to keep it secure, and
        delete them after {HOSTING.logRetention}.
      </p>
      <p>
        Legal basis: Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR. Our legitimate interest is a website that works and stays secure. We do not combine
        this data with other data and do not use it to identify you.
      </p>

      <h2 id="browser">3. The Magnetite browser</h2>
      <p>
        Magnetite runs on your device. The Magnetite project receives no data from it: no telemetry, no usage statistics, no
        account. Nothing below goes to us.
      </p>

      <h3>What stays on your device</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Spaces, tabs, the archive, history, settings and cached site icons live in Magnetite’s data folder, which only your
          user account can read.
        </li>
        <li>
          Private profiles live in memory. Their tabs are not saved, synced or archived, and their site icons are not cached.
        </li>
        <li>
          Importing from another browser (Arc, Chromium-based browsers, Firefox, Zen) reads that browser’s files on your
          computer when you click Import, and Arc’s sidebar also on first start. Magnetite keeps only what ends up in the
          sidebar: addresses, titles and folder names. Nothing is uploaded, and passwords and cookies are never read.
        </li>
        <li>The sidebar shows icons only of sites you have opened; it never fetches icons of other sites.</li>
        <li>Magnetite keeps your last screenshot in memory only, until you dismiss or replace it.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="updates">Checking for updates</h3>
      <p>
        At start and once a day, Magnetite asks GitHub Releases (GitHub, Inc., USA) whether a new signed version exists. It
        is a plain request for the latest release, straight from your computer to GitHub: GitHub sees your IP address and
        the browser’s user agent, and nothing else is sent. You can turn the check off in Settings → About.
      </p>

      <h3>Sync</h3>
      <p>
        Sync is off until you turn it on. Then it talks to the server you pick in Settings → Sync: Magnetite’s free server
        at <code>{SYNC.host}</code> or one you run yourself. Your devices encrypt everything before upload, and the 24-word
        sync code never leaves them. A <code>magnetite-sync</code> server stores:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>the sync chain’s id, which is an anonymous public key;</li>
        <li>the day the chain was created and the day it was last used;</li>
        <li>opaque collection ids and version numbers;</li>
        <li>the encrypted, padded data.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        No email address, password, device name, IP address or readable content. The server does not log requests. Chains
        unused for 400 days are deleted (the operator can change this), and “Delete sync chain” in Settings erases one at
        once. The chain’s key and its two dates are pseudonymous data: they are not tied to your name or email, but they
        stay the same for your devices. If you run your own server, you are responsible for it and for what any reverse
        proxy in front of it logs. Details: <a href={docs.syncServer}>sync server README</a>, <a href={docs.syncAdr}
          >sync design</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h3>Magnetite’s sync server</h3>
      <p>
        <code>{SYNC.host}</code> is run by the controller named in section 1 and hosted by {SYNC.hosting}, which processes the
        data on our behalf under a data processing agreement (Art. 28 GDPR). To deliver the service and protect it from
        abuse, the reverse proxy in front of it processes your IP address while a request runs and to limit how many
        requests one address can make; it writes no access logs. The legal basis is our legitimate interest in running the
        service securely (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR). Backups of the database are kept for {SYNC.backupDays} days, so a deleted
        chain is gone from them after that. Using the server needs no sign-up. How it is set up: <a href={docs.syncPublic}
          >public instance</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h2 id="rights">4. Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        Under the GDPR you have the right of access (Art. 15), rectification (Art. 16), erasure (Art. 17), restriction of
        processing (Art. 18), data portability (Art. 20) and to object (Art. 21). Write to
        <a href={privacyMail}>{LEGAL.privacyEmail}</a>. You also have the right to lodge a complaint with a data protection
        supervisory authority (Art. 77): the one responsible for us, the {LEGAL.authority}, or the one in the EU Member State
        where you live or work.
      </p>

      <h2 id="changes">5. Changes</h2>
      <p>
        We update this policy when the website or the browser changes. The current version is always on this page, and its
        history is public in the <a href={WEBSITE_REPO}>website’s repository</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
